# Chapter Ten — The Safety Copy

= Draft: dramatic conversation cut 0.3. Cut by a third after the pass-2 review. Three documents where there were nine. Marian is in the room when June is heard, and Robert drove. The production reveals artistic lineage and responsibility, not the mechanics of Paul's death. No interaction branches yet.

## Scene 27 — Bungalow Six

= Present: Henry, Nell, Ruth, Marian, and Miriam Havel. Robert is in the car outside the gate.

= Immediate pressure: Ward has spoken to Havel first. Havel will show what she must and surrender nothing.

INT./EXT. TREMONT ANNEX, BUNGALOW SIX - MORNING

From the street, Tremont Annex is a white warehouse behind a gate too narrow for glamour. Inside, a rain-dark street ends at unpainted plywood. A third-floor bedroom window stands above no house.

At nine fifteen Friday morning, a production report is clipped beside the gate:

!A SINGLE LIGHT

!ARTHUR RUSK — DIRECTOR

!PRODUCTION ACCOUNT 418-R

Marian came because Nell asked her. Robert drove, and is waiting in the car outside the gate with the engine off, because that is what he does.

Miriam Havel, the associate producer, meets them outside Bungalow Six. She is forty-four. She does not offer her hand until she knows which of the three women is Nell.

HAVEL
Detective Ward left at eight fifty. He copied what he wanted and asked us to preserve the rest. Nothing leaves my custody.

HENRY
I have not asked you to.

HAVEL
Not yet.

She opens the bungalow door. The front office has been cleared except for one table, four folding chairs, and a gray production file marked 418-R.

HAVEL
Detective Ward said your concern is June Avery's property.

NELL
My concern is my mother. The property is how I was admitted.

Havel takes that in.

HAVEL
Then ask first.

NELL
Do you have her recordings?

HAVEL
Working transfers of six rehearsal tapes, and photostats of the folder that came with them. Paul Ellery delivered them in two lots. The originals went back to him eleven weeks before the supper.

Ruth sits. Nell does not. Marian stands behind Nell's chair with her gloves in one hand.

NELL
What did you believe he was authorized to sell?

HAVEL
Reference material from an abandoned Arthur Rusk picture.

NELL
That was not the question.

Havel opens the file to a one-page source declaration. It is signed only by Paul.

!SOURCE AUTHORITY: THROUGH FAMILY

!RIGHTS CONTACT: P. ELLERY

Below it, in Havel's hand: VERIFICATION PENDING.

NELL
Which family?

HAVEL
He said his former wife controlled her mother's papers.

MARIAN
She did not control them. They were in my garage for eleven years.

HAVEL
We asked him for her signature.

NELL
You did not ask me.

HAVEL
No.

RUTH
What did pending prevent?

HAVEL
At first? The shooting draft. We called what we were doing a test. Then the scene worked, and we had a crew waiting on pages and one room that finally made sense, and pending stayed on the form.

NELL
It became yes.

HAVEL
Yes.

HENRY
You were the woman who called Lydia Shaw.

HAVEL
Yes. Her name was beside the wardrobe cue on an old call sheet. I wanted to know why the cue had survived into every version. I did not want to open an ownership question over the telephone.

NELL
You opened it. You just did not stay for the answer.

HAVEL
Yes.

HENRY
The message at the Kenton Rooms said Thursday at ten.

HAVEL
That was Paul's appointment here. Yesterday. He was bringing a corrected declaration, not more material. He said he was correcting more than one account.

She turns to a set of dialogue vouchers. Each is payable to Paul. Each is marked OUTSIDE DIALOGUE — R.E.

Ruth looks at the initials.

HAVEL
Ward said Paul paid you for pages. He did not say you were R.E.

RUTH
Those are my initials.

HAVEL
You wrote the bedroom pass.

RUTH
I wrote several passes without being told there was a bedroom.

For the first time, Havel loses the order in which she meant to speak.

HAVEL
I have wanted to meet you for four months. Paul said you required anonymity. No calls, no meetings, no credit. Everything through him.

RUTH
He said a name would end the arrangement.

HAVEL
It might have improved it.

RUTH
For whom?

Havel looks at the vouchers. Paul received every payment.

NELL
He kept each of you from asking the other.

RUTH
Yes.

## Scene 28 — One bedroom

INT. TREMONT ANNEX, BEDROOM SET - MORNING

NELL
You said you tested a scene. Show me.

Havel takes a ring of keys from the file drawer and the gray file under her arm.

HAVEL
The crew is off the stage until noon. You stay with me.

They cross one loading lane and enter the false house through its missing exterior wall.

The bedroom is nearly complete. Pale wallpaper stops two feet above the floor. A bed faces a dark wardrobe. Beyond it, a practical door opens onto three feet of hallway and then the soundstage.

Marian stops before anyone.

MARIAN
The bed was against the other wall.

HAVEL
In the picture?

MARIAN
In her room. They moved it for the camera. She hated that. She said she kept reaching for the lamp on the wrong side.

Nobody answers. Havel unlocks a narrow reference cabinet and lays three clipped packets on the dresser.

HAVEL
*A Single Light* is not *Private Weather*. The story is different. The people are different. The ending is different.

NELL
The room is not.

HAVEL
No.

The first packet is a photostat of the blue folder. The bedroom page carries the heading Henry saw at Lydia's:

!INT. BEDROOM — NIGHT

In the margin is the movement Lydia recorded: WOMAN CLOSES WARDROBE BEFORE ANSWERING MAN IN HALL. Near the bottom is June's sentence. A later page transcribes what happened after one take. Beside JUNE — NOT SCENE are two lines:

!COMING INDOORS ISN'T COMING BACK.

!I ONLY SAID HE COULD COME IN.

The second packet is an early 418-R draft. It keeps the movement and the sentence almost exactly. It also turns June's first after-take line into dialogue. No writer is named.

The third is marked R.E. — DIALOGUE PASS. Ruth's version moves June's sentence into the middle of the scene, removes six explanatory lines, and reshapes the after-take remark into a final exchange.

HAVEL
That ending is why the room is still standing.

RUTH
It was three lines when I sent it.

HAVEL
Rusk cut the man's last question.

RUTH
Then the woman's answer is harsher.

HAVEL
He thought the silence helped her.

RUTH
It helps him.

Havel looks at her with undisguised professional pleasure.

HAVEL
I knew you would say that.

Ruth turns away from the page.

NELL
Which part belongs to my mother?

HAVEL
The action at the wardrobe. The sentence. The emotional premise of the scene.

NELL
Which part belongs to Ruth?

HAVEL
The structure after the lie. The withheld entrance. The last exchange.

NELL
And you shot both.

HAVEL
Yes.

Henry remains at the edge of the set.

HENRY
Ruth, when did you know the pages came from more than an unfinished script?

RUTH
On the fifth packet. There was a false start in the margin. A cough. Someone asking whether the tape was running. It read like a private recording. I asked Paul.

NELL
And?

RUTH
He said it was cleared.

NELL
By whom?

RUTH
I did not ask.

NELL
There was a sixth packet.

RUTH
Yes.

HENRY
Why did you continue?

RUTH
The check came.

She looks at her own ending on the page.

RUTH
And I was good at it.

HAVEL
You were.

NELL
Being good at it did not give you permission.

RUTH
No.

## Scene 29 — The safety copy

INT. TREMONT ANNEX, REFERENCE ROOM - DAY

A curtained reference room shares one wall with the set. Havel opens it and switches on a small projector.

HAVEL
Rusk kept one workprint from 1958. Paul did not supply this.

She threads a short reel already waiting in a can. The old image appears on the wall.

The frame is black and white, restless with grain. A pale scratch descends near the right edge. The image jumps at an old splice.

June Avery stands beside a wardrobe in a room with the same proportions as the set. A man's voice comes from the hall. June closes the wardrobe before she turns toward him.

JUNE (ON FILM)
Don't improve it just because it's over.

The image jumps. June looks past the man and toward someone beside the camera. The fragment ends.

Marian has not sat down. She is looking at the wall where the image was.

MARIAN
She was looking at me.

NELL
You were there?

MARIAN
I held her coat. Every day. Somebody had to.

Havel stops the projector and switches on a quarter-inch tape machine beneath it. The safety transfer turns behind a clear plastic cover.

There is no image. June coughs. A man asks whether the tape is still running.

JUNE (ON TAPE)
Coming indoors isn't coming back. I only said he could come in.

Someone laughs once. June does not.

MARIAN
Stop it.

Havel stops the tape. The reel turns down and is still. Nobody speaks. Marian has her gloves in both hands.

MARIAN
I'm sorry. I asked Lydia to burn that. Twice. So that nobody would hear her after she could not tell them no.

NELL
I heard her.

MARIAN
I know.

She looks at the machine.

MARIAN
Go on.

HAVEL
There is only the door.

MARIAN
Then let it close.

Havel starts the tape. The sound of a door closing. Then hiss. Havel stops it and changes reels on the projector.

The recent daily is in color. The wallpaper, dress, and performers are different. The woman crosses the room on the same path. She closes the wardrobe before she answers.

WOMAN (ON FILM)
Don't improve it just because it's over.

The man remains beyond the door.

MAN (ON FILM)
I came back.

WOMAN (ON FILM)
You came indoors.

She does not open the wardrobe or the door. The take ends.

MARIAN
She's doing it wrong.

NELL
She's doing it the same.

MARIAN
That's what's wrong.

NELL
The new ending is better.

Ruth looks at her.

RUTH
Don't.

NELL
I am not helping you.

RUTH
I know.

HAVEL
The first 418-R draft ended at June's sentence. Ruth made the scene continue without letting the man enter. That changed the rest of the picture.

NELL
And you believe that makes the beginning free.

HAVEL
No. I believe it makes the problem more expensive than removal.

HENRY
If someone took every page Paul carried on Wednesday, what would you lose?

HAVEL
Nothing from the picture that we could not replace. The transfers are here. The pages are in nine copies.

NELL
Then why did you need him Thursday?

HAVEL
For the corrected declaration.

NELL
Ward read Paul's letter to me on the telephone this morning. Paul admitted taking the box and said he had come to return it. He did not name this picture, Ruth, or any of you. He wrote that he would tell me the rest himself.

HENRY
What was he correcting?

HAVEL
"Through family." He said he would withdraw his claim of authority, identify the rights holder, name the outside writer, and record what he had told each of us.

RUTH
Did he give you my name?

HAVEL
No. He said he would.

NELL
Mine?

HAVEL
He said the authority was not his and he would bring the person it belonged to after he spoke to her.

Nell looks at the old reel in its open can.

NELL
He meant me.

HAVEL
That is what I understand now.

Henry turns back several pages in his notebook. At the top of one is the first question he wrote in his office:

WHAT DID PAUL BRING BACK?

He closes the notebook on the question.

## Scene 30 — A name on the page

INT. TREMONT ANNEX, REFERENCE ROOM - DAY

The reference room is quiet after the projector stops.

HAVEL
We can correct this. June Avery receives source and performance credit. Ruth receives dialogue credit. We compensate the estate and preserve every version.

NELL
You have done all of that except ask.

HAVEL
I am asking.

NELL
You are offering me the answer you need.

HAVEL
The bedroom is scheduled Monday. If we lose the stage, the delay may finish the picture.

NELL
Then preserve it. Do not use another word or recording until the source and the permission are established.

HAVEL
For how long?

NELL
I don't know.

Havel starts to answer and stops. It is the first answer Nell has given her that cannot be placed on a schedule.

Ruth touches the R.E. voucher on the dresser without lifting it.

RUTH
Write my name on the record.

HAVEL
For credit?

RUTH
For the record.

HAVEL
What name?

RUTH
Ruth Ellery.

Havel takes a blank production memorandum from the gray file. She writes the date, the time, and one sentence:

RUTH ELLERY VOLUNTARILY IDENTIFIES HERSELF AS R.E., AUTHOR OF THE DIALOGUE PASSES.

She signs beneath it and turns it toward Ruth. Ruth signs. The original voucher remains unchanged.

NELL
Do you still have the copies you sent Paul?

RUTH
My carbons. Most of the covers.

NELL
The one where you asked about the recording?

RUTH
If it is where I left it.

HENRY
Where?

RUTH
My office.

Marian puts her gloves on, one and then the other, and goes out ahead of them to the car, where Robert has been sitting for two hours with the engine off. Through the gap in the curtain Henry sees her get in, and sees Robert not ask, and sees her put her hand on his arm anyway.

Havel returns to the projector and opens its gate to remove the reel. For one moment, bare light crosses the bedroom wall. Without the old image, the pale scratch disappears.

The wardrobe door remains closed.
